          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A claim came back asking for more specificity on a three-character code. Is that always a reasonable request?",
          "answer": "Requesting more specificity on a three-character ICD-10 code is generally reasonable. Three-character codes are often considered too broad and may not provide enough detail about a patient's condition. The guidelines for coding emphasize the need for specificity to ensure accurate representation of the diagnosis for billing and treatment purposes.\n\nIn many cases, payers might require a more detailed four- or five-character code to properly process claims. This helps to clarify the exact nature of the condition and supports appropriate health care management.\n\nWhile not every claim will require additional specificity, it is common practice to seek clarification when needed. It's important to be prepared to provide the most precise diagnostic code available based on the clinical details whenever possible.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
